incident 7bc2d656-019a-4e16-8997-8fddb2b331c4  state triaging  anchor 21:17:39
triage: 6 services, severity critical, start from frontend, 5 unmeasured edge(s) crossed

states: triaging -> planning -> investigating -> synthesizing -> proposing
trajectory: 1eeef6cd-c85b-42d1-8291-49469394e69f
triage judged: investigate (medium confidence, suspects unknown)
  A critical alert on the user-facing frontend with no open incidents to attribute it to cannot be dismissed. Only the seed alerted, so the cause could be local to frontend or an unmeasured downstream dependency; no fault class is suggested yet.
investigation: 2 round(s), 6 dispatch(es), 20 step(s)

=== PROPOSAL ===
  action      : revert_config (config_revert)
  target      : frontend
  expect      : Restoring frontend's prior resource config (limits.cpu 1 / requests.cpu 100m, undoing the 500m/500m edit at 21:10:10 [tr_ec5e968a606a]) and recreating the container should let frontend complete requests again. Observable as: the frontend span rate denominator becoming defined and non-zero in every 5m interval (no repeat of the three no-span intervals), and the frontend Loki stream emitting request lines beyond a single cold-start block instead of going silent as it did after 21:10:11.
  within      : 600s
  risk        : If the diagnosis is wrong this is a wasted recreate of the alerted user-facing service, dropping in-flight requests and its in-process cache. Restoring a 1-core ceiling also lets frontend push more load downstream; if the real fault is a saturated dependency, this deepens it. It also reverses a platform-automation action, which may be re-applied and flap unless that automation is paused.
  blast radius: frontend is recreated: external callers see resets for the restart duration. Its five downstream dependencies (profile, recommendation, reservation, search, user) see traffic stop, then resume at possibly higher volume.
  if wrong    : Falsified if, 10 minutes after the revert and its cold start, frontend span rate is still undefined/zero and Loki still shows nothing past the startup block. Note two live doubts: container-level throttling, restart and OOM data were never returned [tr_d2052c256486], so starvation is inferred not measured; and Tempo returned nothing for the whole window with a truncated flag [tr_adf228881c31], so if logs show traffic while span metrics stay empty, suspect the telemetry pipeline rather than a failed fix.
  rests on    : tr_ec5e968a606a, tr_44133780e673, tr_a1e1555af34a
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

context: 23979 pushed / 2495 pulled (pull rate 0.0942), 2 section(s) dropped

=== VERDICT ===
  fault class : resource_exhaustion
  fix class   : config_revert
  confidence  : medium
  evidence    : tr_ec5e968a606a, tr_44133780e673, tr_a1e1555af34a, tr_d2052c256486, tr_adf228881c31, tr_eacfd29a4cab
  root cause  : Platform automation halved the frontend workload's CPU ceiling (limits.cpu 1 -> 500m, request raised to match) about seven minutes before onset [tr_ec5e968a606a]. The edit recreated the pod: a full cold-start sequence appears at 21:10:11, one second after the change, with all five downstream gRPC clients connecting cleanly [tr_44133780e673]. From that point the frontend goes quiet — no further log output [tr_44133780e673], zero error spans across 22 samples [tr_a1e1555af34a, tr_d2052c256486], and three intervals with no spans at all, meaning it served nothing in those intervals [tr_a1e1555af34a, tr_d2052c256486]. That is CPU starvation under the new ceiling: requests stall rather than fail, so callers hang while the frontend reports no errors. The limit edit is how it started; exhaustion is the failing mechanism.
  OPEN        : No container-level data was ever returned — CPU throttling ratio, restart counts and OOM/eviction events were requested but absent [tr_d2052c256486]; throttling is inferred, not measured.
  OPEN        : Why frontend traces are missing for the entire window including the pre-incident baseline, and why a zero-result query was flagged truncated [tr_adf228881c31] — a broken telemetry pipeline would undermine the 'no spans means no traffic' inference.
  OPEN        : What the 21:10:03 CheckAvailability burst actually hit; the log line carries no peer or status code and reservation's logs are empty under that filter [tr_44133780e673, tr_eacfd29a4cab].

